    </answer>
    <solution><p>The function <m>\displaystyle y=\frac{x^{5}}{\sqrt{x}+5x^{10}}</m> is a quotient of two functions, say <m>f(x)= x^{5}</m> and <m>g(x)=\sqrt{x}+5x^{10}</m>.  Then taking the derivatives we get <m>f'(x)=5x^{4}</m> and <m>g'(x)=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}+50x^{9}</m>.  Finally, the quotient rule says that 
<me>\left[\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right]'=\frac{g(x)f'(x)-f(x)g'(x)}{\left[g(x)\right]^2}</me>
so <me>y'= \frac{(\sqrt{x}+5x^{10}) \cdot (5x^{4})  - (x^{5})\cdot \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}+50x^{9}}{(\sqrt{x}+5x^{10})^2}.</me></p>
</solution>
